\section{Operator Valued Distributions}

The local field operator $\Phi\left(  \mathbf{r}\right)  $ is defined by
\begin{equation}
\Phi\left(  \mathbf{r}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\infty}\varphi_{j}\left(
\mathbf{r}\right)  a_{j}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\infty}\varphi_{j}\left(
\mathbf{r}\right)  \Phi\left(  \varphi_{j}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where the functions $\left\{  \left.  \varphi_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
\infty\right\}  $ form a basis and $\in C^{1}$ in that one define the current
density $\mathbf{j}\left(  \mathbf{r}\right)  $.

\subsection{Nuclear Space}

A nuclear space is a topological vector space with many of the properties of
finite-dimensional vector spaces. The topology on them can be defined by a
family of seminorms whose unit balls decrease rapidly in size. Vector spaces
whose elements are "smooth" in some sense tend to be nuclear spaces; a typical
example of a nuclear space is the set of smooth functions on a compact manifold.

\begin{itemize}
\item Definition 1: A nuclear space is a locally convex topological vector
space such that for any seminorm $p$ we can find a larger seminorm $q$ so that
the natural map from $V_{q}$ to $V_{p}$ is nuclear.

Informally, this means that whenever we are given the unit ball of some
seminorm, we can find a "much smaller" unit ball of another seminorm inside
it, or that any neighborhood of 0 contains a "much smaller" neighborhood. It
is not necessary to check this condition for all seminorms $p$; it is
sufficient to check it for a set of seminorms that generate the topology, in
other words, a set of seminorms that are a subbase for the topology.

\qquad Instead of using arbitrary Banach spaces and nuclear operators, we can
give a definition in terms of Hilbert spaces and trace class operators, which
are easier to understand. (On Hilbert spaces nuclear operators are often
called trace class operators.) We will say that a seminorm $p$ is a Hilbert
seminorm if $V_{p}$ is a Hilbert space, or equivalently if $p$ comes from a
sesquilinear positive semidefinite form on V.

\item Definition 2: A nuclear space is a topological vector space with a
topology defined by a family of Hilbert seminorms, such that for any Hilbert
seminorm $p$ we can find a larger Hilbert seminorm $q$ so that the natural map
from $V_{q}$ to $V_{p}$ is trace class.

Some authors prefer to use Hilbert-Schmidt operators rather than trace class
operators. This makes little difference, because any trace class operator is
Hilbert-Schmidt, and the product of two Hilbert-Schmidt operators is of trace class.

\item Definition 3: A nuclear space is a topological vector space with a
topology defined by a family of Hilbert seminorms, such that for any Hilbert
seminorm $p$ we can find a larger Hilbert seminorm $q$ so that the natural map
from $V_{q}$ to $V_{p}$ is Hilbert-Schmidt. 

\begin{itemize}
\item If we are willing to use the concept of a nuclear operator from an
arbitrary locally convex topological vector space to a Banach space, we can
give shorter definitions as follows:
\end{itemize}

\item Definition 4: A nuclear space is a locally convex topological vector
space such that for any seminorm $p$ the natural map from $V$ to $V_{p}$ is nuclear.

\item Definition 5: A nuclear space is a locally convex topological vector
space such that any continuous linear map to a Banach space is nuclear.

\item Definition 6: A nuclear space is a locally convex topological vector
space $A$ such that for any locally convex topological vector space $B$ the
natural map from the projective to the injective tensor product of $A$ and $B$
is an isomorphism.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Examples}

* The space of smooth functions on any compact manifold is nuclear.



* The Schwartz space of smooth functions on $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ for which the
derivatives of all orders are \qquad\qquad\ rapidly decreasing is a nuclear space.



* The space of entire holomorphic functions on the complex plane is nuclear.



* The inductive limit of a sequence of nuclear spaces is nuclear.



* The strong dual of a nuclear Frechet space is nuclear.



* The product of a family of nuclear spaces is nuclear.



* The completion of a nuclear space is nuclear (and in fact a space is nuclear
if and only if its completion is nuclear).

Another example: nuclear space $\Omega$ made up of functions $\varphi\left(
x\right)  \in C^{\infty}$ that vanish more rapidly than any inverse power of
$x$ in the limit $\left\vert x\right\vert $ $\rightarrow$ $\infty$ which
satisfy the infinite set of conditions,%
\[
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\left(  1+\left\vert x\right\vert ^{m}\right)
\left\vert \varphi\left(  x\right)  \right\vert ^{2}dx<\infty;m=0,1,2,\ldots
\]


The functions $\varphi\left(  x\right)  $, which make up $\Omega,$along with
all of  their derivatives vanish more rapidly than any inverse power of $x$ in
the limit $\left\vert x\right\vert $ $\rightarrow$ $\infty$. The extended
space $\Omega^{d}$, which is conjugate to $%
%TCIMACRO{\U{3a9} }%
%BeginExpansion
\Omega
%EndExpansion
$, consists of those functions $\U{3c7} (x)$ for which%
\[
\left(  \U{3c7} ,\varphi\right)  =\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\overline{\chi\left(
x\right)  }\varphi\left(  x\right)  dx<\infty\forall\varphi\in\Omega
\]
In addition to the functions of finite norm, which also lie in $\mathcal{H}$,
$\Omega^{d}$ will contain functions that are unbounded at infinity provided
the divergence is no worse than a power of $x$. Hence $\Omega^{d}$ contains
$e^{ikx}$, which is an eigenfunction of the operator $D=i\frac{d}{dx}$. It
also contains the Dirac delta function, $\U{3b4} (x-\U{3bb} )$ which is an
eigenfunction of the operator Q, defined by $Q\U{3c8} (x)=x\U{3c8} (x)$.

\subsection{Frechet Space}

Fr\'{e}chet spaces, named after Maurice Fr\'{e}chet, are special topological
vector spaces. They are generalizations of Banach spaces (normed vector spaces
which are complete with respect to the metric induced by the norm).
Fr\'{e}chet spaces, in contrast, are locally convex spaces which are complete
with respect to a translation invariant metric.

A topological vector space X is a Fr\'{e}chet space if and only if it
satisfies the following three properties:

* it is complete as a uniform space

* it is locally convex

* its topology can be induced by a translation invariant metric, i.e. a metric
$d:X\times X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ such that $d(x,y)=d(x+a,y+a)$for all
$a,x,y$ in $X$. This means that a subset $U$ of $X$ is open if and only if for
every $u$ in $U$ there exists an $\U{3b5} >0$ such that $\{v:d(u,v)<\U{3b5}
\}$ is a subset of $U$.

Note that there is no natural notion of distance between two points of a
Fr\'{e}chet space: many different translation-invariant metrics may induce the
same topology.

The alternative and somewhat more practical definition is the following: a
topological vector space $X$ is a Fr\'{e}chet space if and only if it
satisfies the following three properties:

* it is complete as a uniform space

* it is a Hausdorff space

* its topology may be induced by a countable family of semi-norms
$||.||k,k=0,1,2,...$ This means that a subset $U$ of $X$ is open if and only
if for every $u$ in $U$ there exists $K\geq0$ and $\U{3b5} >0$ such that
$\{v:||u-v||_{k}<\U{3b5} \forall k\leq K\}$ is a subset of $U$.

A sequence $\left\{  x_{n}\right\}  $ in $X$ converges to $x$ in the
Fr\'{e}chet space defined by a family of semi-norms if and only if it
converges to $x$ with respect to each of the given semi-norms.

\subsection{Gelfand Triple}

Let $\mathcal{L}$ be a TVS and $\mathcal{H}$ a Hilbert space such that
$\mathcal{L}$ is a dense subspace of $\mathcal{H}$ wrt the Hilbert space norm.
The topology on $\mathcal{L}$ is chosen such that it is finer than the Hilbert
space topology and the injection $\iota:\mathcal{L\rightarrow H}$ is
continuous. For
\begin{equation}
\lambda\in\mathcal{L\subset H};\varphi\in\mathcal{H}%
\end{equation}
one has
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left.  \varphi\right\vert \bullet\right\rangle
:\mathcal{L\longrightarrow}\mathbb{C};\varphi\in\mathcal{H},
\end{equation}
thus $\left\langle \varphi\right\vert $ $\in\mathcal{L}^{d}$, (the functional
$\left\langle \varphi\right\vert $ is continuous in the Hilbert space topology
thus is continuous in any finer topology on $\mathcal{L}$ thus belongs to
$\mathcal{L}^{d}$ )$.$ The dual $\mathcal{L}^{d}\supset\mathcal{H}^{d}$ ,
$\mathcal{H}^{d}$ is the set of bounded linear functionals ($\equiv$ to the
set of continuous functionals defined on $\mathcal{H)}$ and can be identified
with $\left\{  \left.  \left\langle \varphi\right\vert \right\vert \varphi
\in\mathcal{H}\right\}  ,$ thus $\mathcal{H=H}^{d}$ and can be regarded as a
dense subspace of $\mathcal{L}^{d}$. In this case $\left\{  \mathcal{L}%
,\mathcal{H},\mathcal{L}^{d}\right\}  $ is called a Gelfand triple $\equiv$
rigged Hilbert space and
\[
\mathcal{L}\subset\mathcal{H}\subset\mathcal{L}^{d}.
\]




\subsection{Generalized Basis}

In a seperable Hilbert space there exists a self adjoint Hilbert-Schmidt
operator
\[
K:\mathcal{H\longrightarrow H},
\]
such that
\[
\ker\left\{  K\right\}  =0,
\]
i.e. $K$ is injective $\Longleftrightarrow0\notin\sigma\left(  K\right)  $,
however it cannot be invertible (thus not surjective). Generalized bases are
associated with operators (bounded and unbounded) that act on $\mathcal{H}$. A
set of vectors $\left\{  \left\vert \zeta\right\rangle \right\}  $ is a
generalized basis for $\mathcal{H}$ if

\subsection{Unbounded Operator Adjoint}

The adjoint operator $a^{\dagger}$ of an unbounded operator $a$ having dense
domain $D\left(  a\right)  $ in $\mathcal{H}$ is defined on the domain
\[
D\left(  a^{\dagger}\right)  =\left\{  \varphi\left\vert \varphi\in
\mathcal{H};\sup_{\psi\in D\left(  a\right)  }\left\Vert a\right\Vert
<\theta\right.  \right\}
\]
by the formula%
\[
\left\langle \left.  a^{\dagger}\varphi\right\vert \psi\right\rangle
=\left\langle \left.  \varphi\right\vert a\psi\right\rangle \forall\psi\in
D\left(  a\right)  ,\varphi\in D\left(  a^{\dagger}\right)  .
\]


If $a=a^{\dagger}$ then it is said to be self adjoint, if $a^{\dagger
}=a^{\dagger\dagger}$ then it is said to be essentially self adjoint, if
$a^{\dagger}\supset a$ then it is hermitian $\equiv$ symmetric.

\subsection{Quadratic Form}

A quadratic form $q$ is a mapping
\begin{align*}
q  & :Q\left(  q\right)  \times Q\left(  q\right)  \rightarrow\mathbb{C}\\
q\left(  \varphi,\psi\right)    & =\left\langle \left.  \varphi\right\vert
\hat{q}\psi\right\rangle ;\varphi,\psi\in Q\left(  q\right)
\end{align*}
where $\hat{q}$ is an operator (not in general bounded) and $Q\left(
q\right)  $ is a dense subspace of $\mathcal{H}$ known as the form domain,
such that $\left\langle \left.  \varphi\right\vert \hat{q}\psi\right\rangle $
is linear in $\psi$ and conjugate linear in $\varphi.$ The domain of the form
$Q\left(  q\right)  $ can be different from the domain $Q\left(  \hat
{q}\right)  $ of the operator $\hat{q}$. If $\left\langle \left.
\varphi\right\vert \hat{q}\psi\right\rangle =\left\langle \left.
\psi\right\vert \hat{q}\varphi\right\rangle ^{\ast}$ then $q$ is said to
hermitian, and if $\left\langle \left.  \varphi\right\vert \hat{q}%
\varphi\right\rangle \geq0$ it is said to be positive. A positive form is said
to be closed iff $Q\left(  q\right)  $ is complete in the form norm%
\[
\left\Vert \varphi\right\Vert _{q}^{2}=\left\langle \left.  \varphi\right\vert
\hat{q}\varphi\right\rangle +\left\Vert \varphi\right\Vert ^{2}.
\]
The form $q$ (and the operator $\hat{q}$) is semibounded if
\[
\left\langle \left.  \varphi\right\vert \hat{q}\varphi\right\rangle
\geq-M\left\Vert \varphi\right\Vert ^{2}%
\]
and
\[
\sup_{\varphi\in\mathcal{H}}\left\{  \frac{\left\langle \left.  \varphi
\right\vert \hat{q}\varphi\right\rangle }{\left\Vert \varphi\right\Vert ^{2}%
}\right\}  =\infty,
\]
which is equivalent to%

\[
\left\langle \left.  \varphi\right\vert \hat{q}_{1}\psi\right\rangle
=\left\langle \left.  \varphi\right\vert \hat{q}\psi\right\rangle
+M\left\langle \left.  \varphi\right\vert \psi\right\rangle
\]
being positive. 

Hermitian forms, in contradistinction to Hermitian operators, need not be
closable. However if they are closeable then they are always the quadratic
form of self-adjoint operator.

\begin{theorem}
If the form $q$ is positive and closed, then it is the form of a unique
self-adjoint, positive operator, the extension $\tilde{q}$ of $\hat{q}.$
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
Every semibounded operator (that defines a closed Hermitian form) has a
self-adjoint extension known as the Freidichs extension.
\end{corollary}


